signatureMoments.design:
When you finish, the static result card is replaced by your own right-aligned bubble (max-width 85%), filled with the game's accent and #141216 text. Inside it:
- the label line ('Solved · 4/7'), 13px Figtree 700;
- the score in 64px Bricolage 800, counting up;
- the share grid in an inset 'quoted message' block with surface background, 12px radius and 8px/12px padding, so colored emoji squares never sit on a colored fill (a 🟨 on a sunflower bubble would vanish).

Secondary text in the bubble is `color-mix(in srgb, #141216 78%, var(--accent))`. That measures at least 5.05:1 on every accent in the set. A muted timestamp '8:14 AM' sits under the bubble. 300ms later the spoiler wall opens and friends' bubbles arrive in a cascade. The thread ends with a centered system pill such as 'You're #2 in Number Hunt today · 6 behind Priya'.

The hub reads as today's thread and opens under a sticky date-divider pill ('Tuesday, Oct 6', 12px Figtree 700, surface-2 pill centered over the wallpaper). The header countdown reads 'New drop in 3:12:08' in Bricolage 600 tabular.

At 00:00 New York time, the Countdown component calls router.refresh() and then:
- the divider crossfades to the new date;
- a system pill appears: '🗞 New puzzles are up';
- every game row gets an accent unread dot;
- yesterday collapses into one tappable recap pill, '👑 Priya won yesterday · you were #3', which opens the final standings;
- yesterday's overall winner wears a small 👑 badge on their avatar all day.

directions.scalesToNewGames:
A new game supplies only its emoji, its accent, a name and an emoji share grid. The platform derives everything else from those:
- the thread avatar;
- the color of your result bubble;
- the unread dot;
- the tab pill;
- the ring segments in the presence tray;
- accent-ink text, via `color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) var(--accent-ink-mix), var(--fg))`, where --accent-ink-mix is 38% in light mode and 100% in dark mode;
- tints, via color-mix at 14%.

Accent guardrail. The six curated accents (#FFC53D, #A6E34B, #3ED598, #5CB8FF, #FF85C2, #B49CFF) all pass these checks, measured:
- #141216 text on the accent: 8.10 to 12.16:1;
- the 38% accent-ink against light bg and surface-2: at least 5.12:1;
- the accent as text on dark surface-2: at least 6.47:1;
- secondary #141216-at-78% text inside accent bubbles: at least 5.44:1.

A one-off accent is allowed if it passes the same assertions in registry.test.ts (ink on accent ≥ 7, accent-ink ≥ 4.5 on bg and surface-2 in both modes, accent ≥ 4.5 on dark surface-2). Accent hexes need relative luminance of roughly 0.32 to 0.75: bright and candy-like, never dark.

Game UIs may use only the semantic tokens plus --accent and --accent-ink. That rules out raw Tailwind palettes like the current sky/rose/emerald in number-hunt.

Conversational games plug into ChatLog and Composer and feel native to the chat. Spatial games (logic grids, maps, charts) get the plain panel. The share grid is required to be emoji text, so it renders the same in our bubbles and in the real group chat.

A new game declares only three things: accent (one of the 6 palette colors), emoji, and an optional 2-5 letter symbol (default: the uppercased first 4 letters of its id). Everything else is derived from the accent with color-mix:
- the card's left bar, emoji tile tint and friend pips;
- the play-screen top rule;
- the leaderboard chip fill and depth bars;
- the result-ticket header;
- --accent-ink: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 60%, black) in light mode, and the raw accent in dark mode.

Verified contrast:
- Black text on every palette accent: 8.3:1 or better.
- Accent as text on the dark surface: 6.7:1 or better.
- Accent-ink as text on the light surface: 5.3:1 to 7.8:1.

The palette avoids the hues reserved for meaning: house amber (platform / you), green (up) and red (down). A future word game, logic grid, map or quote game sits in the plain game panel using the shared kit and body font, so it doesn't inherit any terminal noise. Chart and number games get the aesthetic for free: mono numerals, hairline grids and sparkline strokes already match. If more than 6 games exist, the 7th reuses a palette color, and the symbol plus emoji keep them distinct.
